From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wispr: treat HTTP 204 as a successful online check

wispr_portal_web_result_success() switches on the HTTP status of the online
check and has cases for 200, the 3xx redirects, 400, 404, 408, 505 and
"unknown". 204 is not among them, so it falls through to `default: break;'.

The only path that reports a successful check is portal_manage_success_status(),
reached solely from `case GWEB_HTTP_STATUS_CODE_OK' and only when the response
carries an X-ConnMan-Status header - which is what connman's own
http://ipv4.connman.net/online/status.html used to return. Every endpoint that
is actually reachable today (Google's and Android's generate_204,
connectivity-check.ubuntu.com) answers 204 with no body and no headers, so
wp_context->cb() is never invoked for them.

The result is silent: the request succeeds, no error is recorded on the service,
and the service simply stays in "ready" for ever. Anything keyed off "online"
never fires - including isInternetConnectionAvailable in webos-connman-adapter,
and through it the ActivityManager's "internet" requirement, which holds any
activity carrying it indefinitely.

Measured on qemux86-64 with OnlineCheckIPv4URL = http://www.gstatic.com/generate_204:

  before: wispr_portal_web_result_success() status: 204
          ... no complete_online_check(), service State = ready,
          isInternetConnectionAvailable false, an activity with
          requirements {"internet": true} only ever gets "update", never "start"

  after:  State = online, isInternetConnectionAvailable true,
          the same activity gets "update" then "start"

There is no body and no X-ConnMan-* header to mine on a 204, so report success
directly rather than going through portal_manage_success_status().

Upstream-Status: Pending

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
 src/wispr.c | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/wispr.c b/src/wispr.c
--- a/src/wispr.c
+++ b/src/wispr.c
@@ -1137,6 +1137,25 @@
 				wispr_portal_browser_reply_cb,
 				wp_context->status_url, wp_context);
 		break;
+	case GWEB_HTTP_STATUS_CODE_NO_CONTENT:
+		/*
+		 * A "204 No Content" is an unambiguous "you reached the real
+		 * server, not a captive portal" -- it is what the endpoints
+		 * everyone actually uses reply with (Android's and Google's
+		 * generate_204, connectivity-check.ubuntu.com). Without this
+		 * case a 204 falls through to `default' and the online check
+		 * completion callback is never invoked, so the service sits in
+		 * "ready" for ever with no error recorded, and anything keyed
+		 * off "online" (isInternetConnectionAvailable, and through it
+		 * the ActivityManager's "internet" requirement) never fires.
+		 *
+		 * There is no body and no X-ConnMan-* headers to mine here, so
+		 * report success directly rather than going through
+		 * portal_manage_success_status().
+		 */
+		wp_context->cb(wp_context->service, wp_context->type,
+				true, 0, NULL);
+		break;
 	default:
 		break;
 	}
